#{[17592186077744 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186077748] [17592186078020 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186078022] [17592186078705 :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay equation is proportional to the difference between the limiting value and the current value. With limiting value $40$ and rate constant $20$, it is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}=20(40-y)=800-20y.\n$$\n\nFor $y>40$ this derivative is negative and approaches zero as $y$ approaches $40$."] [17592186079159 :question/worked-solution "Substitute $y=e^{\\lambda x}$ into the equation and divide by the nonzero factor $e^{\\lambda x}$:\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2-5\\lambda+6=(\\lambda-2)(\\lambda-3)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\lambda=2$ or $\\lambda=3$."] [17592186079160 :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2-5\\lambda-6=(\\lambda-6)(\\lambda+1).\n$$\n\nThus all solutions are linear combinations of $e^{6x}$ and $e^{-x}$. The option $e^{5x}$ is not such a solution: substitution gives\n\n$$\n(25-25-6)e^{5x}=-6e^{5x}\\ne0.\n$$"] [17592186079161 :question/worked-solution "The exponential trial solution leads to\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2+9\\lambda+18=(\\lambda+6)(\\lambda+3)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\lambda=-6$ or $\\lambda=-3$."] [17592186079207 :question/worked-solution "The characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2-3\\lambda-10=(\\lambda-5)(\\lambda+2)=0.\n$$\n\nThe two distinct roots are $5$ and $-2$, so the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=Ae^{-2x}+Be^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary real constants."] [17592186084836 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186084841] [17592186086586 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186086588] [17592186086604 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186086608] [17592186087143 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186087148] [17592186087149 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186087153] [17592186087179 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186087184] [17592186087317 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186087319] [17592186087458 :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n    =\n    f(x, y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero, meaning that it satisfies\n\n$$\nf(tx, ty) = f(x, y).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at the right-hand side function in each differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation I is a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$ is *not* a homogeneous function of degree $0.$ Rather, it is a homogeneous function of degree $-1\\mathbin{:}$\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{{t}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}f(x,y)\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation II is *not* a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}\n$$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{ty}{2(tx)}\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot 2x}\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2x}\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation III is a homogenous differential equation.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186088017 :question/worked-solution "The decay model has solution $N(t)=230000e^{-kt}$. Use the measurement after five years:\n\n$$\n23000=230000e^{-5k}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k=\\frac{\\ln10}{5}\\approx0.460517.\n$$\n\nThe continuous decay rate is approximately $46\\%$ per year, to the nearest percent."] [17592186088322 :question/worked-solution "Compare with $\\frac{x^2}{a^2}+\\frac{y^2}{b^2}=1$. Here $a=\\sqrt{10}$ and $b=3$, with $a>b$. The lengths of the major and minor axes are therefore\n\n$$\n2a=2\\sqrt{10},\\qquad2b=6.\n$$"] [17592186088325 :question/worked-solution "The graph crosses the $x$-axis at $\\pm3$ and the $y$-axis at $\\pm4$. Thus the horizontal and vertical semiaxes are $3$ and $4$, respectively. Its equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{3^2}+\\frac{y^2}{4^2}=1,\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{16}=1.\n$$"] [17592186088415 :question/worked-solution "The initial depth is $D(0)=300$ feet. Solving $D^{\\prime}=0.01D$ gives\n\n$$\nD(t)=300e^{0.01t}.\n$$\n\nAfter six years,\n\n$$\nD(6)=300e^{0.06}\\approx318.550964\\text{ feet}.\n$$\n\nRounded to the nearest integer, the depth is $319$ feet."] [17592186088416 :question/worked-solution "The solution is $V(t)=4800e^{kt}$. Apply the value after three years:\n\n$$\n7200=4800e^{3k}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k=\\frac{\\ln(3/2)}3\\approx0.135155.\n$$\n\nThe continuous annual growth rate is $14\\%$, to the nearest percent."] [17592186088421 :question/worked-solution "The solution with the given initial price is\n\n$$\nP(t)=250000e^{0.0325t}.\n$$\n\nAfter $25$ years,\n\n$$\nP(25)=250000e^{0.8125}\\approx563383.6968.\n$$\n\nRounded to the nearest dollar, the price is $\\$563384$."] [17592186088437 :question/answer-fields #edb/ref 17592186395951] [17592186088444 :question/worked-solution "Set $z=y^2$, so $z^{\\prime}=2yy^{\\prime}$. Multiplying the ODE by $2y$ gives\n\n$$\nz^{\\prime}=x+\\frac{3z}{x}.\n$$\n\nOn an interval with $x\\ne0$, multiply $z^{\\prime}-3z/x=x$ by $x^{-3}$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dx}(zx^{-3})=x^{-2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating gives $zx^{-3}=-x^{-1}+K$, hence\n\n$$\ny^2=Kx^3-x^2.\n$$\n\nThe original ODE requires $x\\ne0$ and $y\\ne0$. This implicit expression covers both sign branches on intervals where $Kx^3-x^2>0$."] [17592186088902 :tutorial/content "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $\\color{red}a$ and a vertical radius of length $\\color{blue}b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{{\\color{red}a}^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{{\\color{blue}b}^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\n![](images/92/9227839bbf9a01312c3014d336a88176eecd73aa934fcd4741c11271b1e0bea6.png)\n\nTo demonstrate, let's determine the equation of the following ellipse. The horizontal radius has length $\\color{red}a=3$ and the vertical radius has length $\\color{blue}b=2.$\n\n![](images/fe/fe7dff90438e9571ae599a446e355776fbd36ed4746dd76e6633f3848e1f6bbc.png)\n\nTherefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{{\\color{red}3}}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{{\\color{blue}2}}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186089243 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186089246]}
